      "Value at risk condenses the left tail of a return distribution into one number. A one-day 95% VaR of $10,000 means that, based on the model, losses on a typical day should be no worse than $10,000 nineteen days out of twenty. Put the other way, about one day in twenty is expected to lose more. VaR is widely used by banks and fund managers to set risk limits because it is simple to state and compare.",
      "There are three common ways to estimate it. Historical VaR takes the actual percentile of past returns. Parametric VaR assumes returns are normal and uses the mean and standard deviation. The Cornish-Fisher version adjusts the normal estimate for skewness and fat tails, which matter because real market returns have more extreme days than a normal distribution predicts. The methods can give quite different answers for the same data.",
      "The key limitation is that VaR says nothing about how bad losses are beyond the threshold: a 95% VaR is the same whether the worst 5% of days lose a little more or a great deal more. That is the gap conditional VaR fills. QuantTrade AI reports VaR in backtest results and through the copilot’s risk metrics tool, which computes historical, parametric and Cornish-Fisher versions.",
